{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's proximity to the Tennessee River creates drainage challenges that surface solutions can't fully address. The river's influence raises water tables throughout the city, meaning the ground is often saturated before storms even begin. When your downspouts dump water a few feet from your foundation, that water has nowhere to go — it pools against your foundation wall.</p>

<p>Morgan County's red clay soil compounds the problem. Clay doesn't absorb water well, so even water directed away from your home often flows back toward the foundation rather than soaking in. Surface extensions and splash blocks provide minimal protection in these conditions.</p>

<p>We see the results throughout Decatur: wet basements, crawlspace moisture problems, foundation cracks, and mold issues. Many homeowners have tried multiple surface solutions without success. The water table is simply too high and the soil too dense for surface drainage to work effectively.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this by routing water far enough away that it can't return. Instead of moving water a few feet and hoping for the best, buried pipes carry it 10, 20, or even 50+ feet to a safe discharge point.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. The water exits at a predetermined location — typically at the property edge, into a storm drain, or into a dry well.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work with your gutter system to create complete water management — from the moment rain hits your roof until it's safely away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage systems throughout Decatur and Morgan County for over 20 years. We understand the Tennessee River's influence on local water tables, how the clay soil behaves, and what drainage solutions actually work in this specific environment.</p>

